O-IOE
Operator-Induced Ontology Expansion

A Framework for Search-Space Expansion, Representational Novelty, and Recursive Machine Discovery

Pantheon-Prime Working Whitepaper • Version 0.1 • August 2026

Abstract
Operator-Induced Ontology Expansion (O-IOE) describes a class of processes in which applying or inventing an operator does more than transform objects already represented by a system: it makes previously unavailable distinctions addressable, thereby expanding the system’s ontology and the search space available to subsequent operations. O-IOE treats novelty as potentially arising from changes in representation, boundary, primitive, composition rule, scale, topology, or operator—not only from recombination inside a fixed vocabulary. The framework separates physical creation from representational creation: a substrate may preexist while a computational object, category, function, or relation is absent from the current ontology. This paper formalizes that distinction, gives a testable algorithm, proposes novelty and fitness criteria, and integrates O-IOE with semantic routing, meta-learning, and recursive machine synthesis.

1. Core Claim
A search system restricted to a fixed ontology can discover only objects expressible in that ontology. A system that can modify its operators and representations can sometimes expose distinctions that were previously unaddressable. Once stabilized and validated, those distinctions become new objects, categories, relations, or operators available to future search.

O_t --phi--> O_(t+1)

O_(t+1) = O_t ∪ ΔO

O-IOE occurs when ΔO contains at least one operationally addressable distinction
that was not expressible as an object/category/relation under the prior interface.

This is not a claim of literal creation of matter from absolute nothing. It is a claim about ontology-relative novelty: X can be absent from the representational and operational vocabulary of a system even when the substrate from which X is later distinguished already exists.

2. Five Useful Kinds of 'Nothing'
• Material absence: the relevant physical substrate is absent.
• Pattern absence: the substrate exists, but the target organization/pattern does not.
• Category absence: a recurring structure exists, but the system has no category that distinguishes it.
• Address absence: a distinction exists conceptually or physically, but there is no stable handle by which the system can retrieve or operate on it.
• Functional absence: components exist, but no composition currently realizes the target behavior.
These should not be conflated. O-IOE primarily concerns category, address, relation, operator, and functional absence.

3. Primitive Relativity
An object is primitive only relative to a representation and an allowed operator set. A prime integer is irreducible under multiplication over positive integers, yet the same number has structure as a bit string, residue class, digit sequence, geometric arrangement, graph node, sum, difference, or program input. The general principle is:

primitive(x | R, Ω) does not imply primitive(x | R', Ω')

Change the representation R or operator family Ω,
and previously atomic objects may become decomposable.

This gives O-IOE a practical search heuristic: when search stalls, do not only search harder inside the current space. Search for a representation or operator under which the current primitives acquire new structure.

4. O-IOE Formalization
Let an ontology at time t be O_t = (E_t, C_t, R_t, Ω_t), where E is the set of addressable entities, C categories, R relations, and Ω available operators. An operator φ acts on both represented objects and, potentially, the representation itself.

phi : (O_t, x) -> (O_(t+1), y)

Strong O-IOE:
   |Expressible(O_(t+1))| > |Expressible(O_t)|

Operational O-IOE:
   exists z such that
       z was not addressable under O_t,
       z is addressable under O_(t+1),
       and z supports a measurable operation/test.

The second criterion is intentionally stricter than merely inventing a label. A new word without a new stable distinction or capability is lexical novelty, not necessarily ontology expansion.

5. Operator-Induced Expansion Loop
operator
  ↓
new distinction
  ↓
new addressable object
  ↓
new category / relation
  ↓
new compositions become legal
  ↓
new operators become definable
  ↓
larger future search space
  ↺

The important compounding effect is that a new operator can change the generator of future candidates. O-IOE therefore values discoveries partly by what they make subsequently expressible.

6. Space Search
Ordinary search chooses candidates x inside a fixed space S. Space Search treats representations themselves as candidates.

Search(x | S)                       # ordinary search

SpaceSearch(S) -> {S1, S2, ...}     # search over representations

OperatorSearch(S) -> {Ω1, Ω2, ...}  # search over legal transformations

SearchRuleSearch -> new ways of generating spaces/operators

Candidate dimensions for Space Search include primitive choice, boundary placement, scale, dimensionality, topology, time representation, observer/reference frame, encoding, ratio, composition rule, and allowed operator.

7. Reference Algorithm
def o_ioe(seed, ontology, fitness, budget):

   frontier = [ontology]
   discoveries = []

   while budget.remaining():

       current = select_promising_space(frontier)

       # Expand not only objects, but how objects can exist for the system.
       variants = mutate_representation(
           current,
           dimensions=[
               "primitive", "boundary", "operator",
               "scale", "topology", "ratio",
               "encoding", "composition_rule",
               "reference_frame"
           ],
       )

       for space in variants:

           rendered = translate(seed, space)

           # Indigo Hammer: challenge apparent atomicity.
           parts = decompose_under_local_operators(rendered, space)
           relations = discover_relations(parts, space)

           candidates = compose(
               parts,
               relations,
               allow_new_categories=True,
               allow_new_operators=True,
           )

           for x in candidates:
               behavior = simulate_or_probe(x)

               if not ontology.can_express(behavior):
                   proposed = induce_distinction(x, behavior)

                   # Naming follows evidence of a stable distinction.
                   if reproducible(proposed) and fitness(proposed) > threshold:
                       ontology = ontology.expand(proposed)
                       discoveries.append(proposed)

                       # Expansion changes the next search frontier.
                       frontier.append(ontology)

       budget.tick()

   return ontology, discoveries

8. Novelty Test
O-IOE should not equate unfamiliar phrasing with genuine novelty. A candidate receives stronger novelty status when it passes progressively harder tests:

• Non-retrieval: it is not merely an existing stored object returned under a new label.
• Non-trivial recombination: its useful behavior is not explained by simply choosing one unchanged source component.
• New addressability: it creates a stable distinction the previous interface could not directly operate on.
• New capability: the expanded ontology supports an operation, prediction, compression, control action, or construction unavailable before.
• Search-space gain: the addition enables materially useful new compositions or operators.
• Validation: the claimed distinction survives reproducibility, simulation, external measurement, formal proof, or another appropriate reality check.
9. Expansion Metrics
Addressability Gain:
AG = number of newly stable, testable distinctions

Operator Gain:
OG = |Ω_(t+1) - Ω_t|

Reachability Gain:
RG = useful reachable states after expansion / before expansion

Compression Gain:
CG = old description cost / new description cost

Validated Capability Gain:
VCG = measured task performance after expansion - before expansion

Expansion Efficiency:
EE = useful expansion / search + complexity cost

Raw combinatorial growth is not automatically valuable. A representation that creates a trillion meaningless combinations has high nominal expansion and low useful expansion.

10. Fitness Route
O-IOE is paired with a context-dependent fitness function so that ontology growth is selected for function rather than novelty alone.

Fitness =
+ function
+ robustness
+ adaptability
+ invariant preservation
+ validated utility
+ optionality
+ learning gain
+ search-space gain

- instability
- complexity
- cost
- semantic loss
- failure
- lock-in
- downstream risk

Fitness weights are themselves calibratable. When predicted utility repeatedly diverges from observed utility, the evaluator should update its weighting rather than merely blaming individual candidates.

11. Cross-Domain Transduction
A major source of candidate operators is preexisting functional structure in other domains. The safe and productive abstraction is not 'biology and mechanics are literally the same.' It is that distinct substrates can sometimes instantiate similar relational patterns.

source phenomenon
   ↓
strip substrate-specific details
   ↓
extract candidate relational invariant
   ↓
translate to target representation
   ↓
instantiate
   ↓
test
   ↓
retain only what survives

Examples include threshold activation (neurons → software/control), distributed gradient following (roots/slime molds → search/logistics), negative feedback (physiology/governors → control), selective coupling (clutches/gating → computation), and ratio transformation (gears → resolution/leverage). Physical chemistry and material constraints remain part of validation; topology alone does not guarantee equivalent behavior.

12. Worked Example: From 'Noise' to an Addressable Signal
Suppose a sensor stream is treated as undifferentiated variation. A new transform partitions the stream by phase relation rather than amplitude. Repeated phase structures become visible, a stable classifier is induced, and the classifier enables prediction that the previous representation could not perform. The underlying photons or measurements were not created. The operational object—the phase-defined signal class—was absent from the prior ontology.

O0:
   entities = amplitude samples
   operators = threshold, average

phi:
   phase_relation_transform

O1:
   entities += phase motifs
   relations += phase coupling
   operators += motif detector

Test:
   prediction(O1) > prediction(O0)

If reproducible, this is an O-IOE event.

13. Worked Example: Primitive Fracture
A system treats a component P as atomic. Search repeatedly stalls. Space Search re-renders P under a graph representation. P decomposes into three recurring relations. Those relations can be independently recombined with other components, producing a new functional assembly. The novelty was enabled not by adding more P-like objects, but by changing what counted as a primitive.

14. Relationship to Existing Fields
O-IOE overlaps with established ideas rather than replacing them. Relevant neighboring concepts include representation learning, feature construction, abstraction, program synthesis, automated scientific discovery, evolutionary computation, ontology engineering, concept formation, category learning, active learning, meta-learning, state-space discovery, and mathematical changes of basis. The proposed contribution is the explicit unification of operator-driven representational change with ontology expansion and recursive search-space growth, plus a practical architecture for measuring and reusing such events.

A literature review is required before making priority claims such as 'first' or 'unprecedented.' O-IOE should be presented as proposed terminology/framework unless comparative scholarship establishes stronger novelty.

