The recursion should stop when marginal validated capability or compression gain falls below the cost of added complexity. Meta-levels must earn their existence.

17. Failure Modes and Falsification
• Renaming illusion: a new label is mistaken for a new ontology.
• Metaphor leakage: properties specific to a source substrate are assumed to survive translation.
• Combinatorial inflation: search-space size grows without useful reachability.
• Coherence trap: an internally elegant expansion fails external validation.
• Over-fracture: useful primitives are decomposed until the representation becomes less efficient.
• Category hallucination: noise is promoted into a stable class without reproducibility.
• Recursive bloat: meta-search consumes more resources than its discoveries justify.
A claimed O-IOE event is weakened or falsified if the supposed new distinction can be represented without loss in the prior ontology, if it produces no measurable new operation or compression, or if the effect disappears under replication.

18. Minimal Experimental Protocol
1. Freeze baseline ontology O0.
2. Define task set T and evaluation metrics.
3. Measure baseline capability C0.
4. Permit operator/representation mutation.
5. Record candidate ontology expansions.
6. Require reproducible addressability.
7. Re-run the same task set under O1.
8. Measure C1 and search-space/reachability changes.
9. Ablate the proposed new distinction/operator.
10. Test whether gains disappear.
11. Retain successful expansion as a Seal/fossil.
12. Repeat on novel tasks to measure transfer.

19. Research Questions
• Can useful ontology expansion be distinguished automatically from arbitrary feature proliferation?
• Which operator families most reliably expose new addressable structure?
• Can search-space expansion be predicted before expensive exploration?
• How should systems trade ontology growth against compression and interpretability?
• Can repeated O-IOE events produce transferable meta-operators for scientific or engineering discovery?
• How often does a newly induced category enable genuinely new operators rather than merely new labels?
• Can negative results and failed translations systematically improve the ontology generator?
20. Claims Discipline
This framework supports three levels of claim. Established: representations and operator choices change what distinctions and computations are convenient or expressible. Proposed: O-IOE is a useful unified abstraction for operator-driven ontology expansion and recursive novelty search. Empirical: any claim that O-IOE improves discovery, agency, reasoning, or machine synthesis must be demonstrated by controlled comparisons, executable implementations, and external validation.

21. Compact Definition
O-IOE (Operator-Induced Ontology Expansion):

A process in which applying, inventing, or changing an operator
causes a system to acquire a stable and operationally useful
distinction, entity, category, relation, or operator that was not
addressable under its prior representational interface, thereby
expanding the useful search space available to subsequent operations.

