23. Conclusion
O-IOE reframes novelty generation as more than sampling unusual combinations from a fixed vocabulary. The stronger move is to alter the vocabulary-producing machinery itself. By searching over representations, primitives, boundaries, relations, and operators, a system can sometimes make previously unavailable structures operationally visible. When those structures survive fitness and reality tests, they become new reusable components of future search. In this sense, the highest-leverage discovery may not be a new answer inside the map, but an operation that changes what the map is capable of containing.

